A||N||4||FieroFanaticus2||HELP HELP HELP HELP||||1||
Z||000000||FieroFanaticus2||11-23-2001||03:16 PM||||Ok, so it's probably not as urgent as I made it seem.  My car has been sitting for a while and I went to take 'er for a drive the other day.  When I got in and pushed the clutch down it had no pressure behind it and the shifter wouldn't budge.  So, I get out and look to see if there was any fluid in the reservour.  There wasn't.  So I filled it up with some DOT 3 Brake fluid and pumped the clutch until there was some pressure and it would shift.  It worked and the car is shifting fine, but now the clutch engages much sooner than before.  Almost as soon as I start to release the clutch it engages, when before it would be almost half way up.  So now I'm thinking maybe there is a leak in the slave or master cyl.?  Maybe I just need to bleed the system?  I know there is probably some air floating around in there since the reservour was emtpy and I pumped the clutch a few times while it was empty, and then again when I filled it.  What do you guys think?  Bleed the slave and see what happens?  Where do I look for leakage?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Voytek||11-23-2001||04:25 PM||||You've got a leak and some air in the system.<P>Yes, you do need to bleed it at some point, whether you find the leak or not.<P>For now, I'd check the obvious places - slave cylinder (at the hose connection and at the rod/piston) and the master cylinder (same, at the hose and piston).  On the master cylinder, if it leaks, it may be leaking inside the car at the wall (right where the pedal / rod goes inside the wall).  I've had that happen to me before.  I couldn't find the leak - turned out the fluid was being absorbed by the insulation under the carpet.<P>If still nothing, check your lines front to back.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||11-23-2001||04:26 PM||||Check the rubber boots on the slave and master cylinders. Usually you will see it on the ground if its leaking badly from the slave.<P>Bleeding the system will take care of a symptom only temporarily but not the problem. <P>Hope that helps!||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroFanaticus2||11-23-2001||04:46 PM||||Ok, Well, all this happened about 1 1/2 - 2 months ago.  The Fiero Has been sitting ever since.  I just went out a checked it again.  It doesn't seem to be leaking (Judging by the fluid reservour still being full), but the clutch is still limp.  Is this something that should be looked at immeadiatly, or is it ok to sit for the winter and checked out in the spring.  I go out about once a month and start it up for a while during the winter just to get things moving around a little.  Is any damage going to occure because of some air being in there??<P>Thanks for you're help guys.  You've helped turn an auto illiterate dweeb into someone who actually feels confident doing his own work on the Car.  Just wanted to let you know how appreciated it is!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||REDHOTT88||11-24-2001||09:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroFanaticus2:<BR><B>Ok, Well, all this happened about 1 1/2 - 2 months ago.  The Fiero Has been sitting ever since.  I just went out a checked it again.  It doesn't seem to be leaking (Judging by the fluid reservour still being full), but the clutch is still limp.  Is this something that should be looked at immeadiatly, or is it ok to sit for the winter and checked out in the spring.  I go out about once a month and start it up for a while during the winter just to get things moving around a little.  Is any damage going to occure because of some air being in there??<P>Thanks for you're help guys.  You've helped turn an auto illiterate dweeb into someone who actually feels confident doing his own work on the Car.  Just wanted to let you know how appreciated it is!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Bump<P><P>------------------<BR>1988 Formula 2.8L <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (red & shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||1
